.TH TPMD 1 "2026" "tpmd" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpmd \- local key agent
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpmd
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpmd
owns private signing keys and signs on behalf of local applications over a
local socket. A consumer never sees key material: it asks for a signature and
gets one, or gets a refusal that says why.
.PP
Keys live at a
.IR "security level" ,
weakest first:
.BR file ,
.BR keyring ,
.B sealed
and
.BR hardware .
The daemon selects the strongest level the machine can actually reach, and
refuses to serve anything below the floor it was started with rather than
quietly handing out a weaker key.
.PP
On Linux the daemon runs in one of two modes. A
.I system
daemon serves every local user and authorises each caller from kernel peer
credentials; a
.I user
agent serves only the account that started it and is normally started on
demand by that account. The two use different sockets and different stores.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Run the agent in the foreground. This is what the service runs; it does not
daemonise itself.
.TP
.B status
Ask a running agent whether it is reachable and what it can do: the backend it
chose, the level it achieved, and whether its store is open.
.TP
.B backend
Show, change or forget which backend this machine uses. The first successful
start records the choice, and later starts keep it, so a machine does not
silently change custody after a reboot.
.RS
.TP
.B backend show
Print the recorded choice and the reason for it. The default.
.TP
.BI "backend set " name " \-\-yes"
Choose a backend deliberately, replacing the recorded one. It can re-key the
device, hence the confirmation.
.TP
.B backend clear
Forget the choice, so the next start runs the selection ladder again.
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.SS serve
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Bind this endpoint instead of the platform default. On Unix in system mode the
default is
.IR /run/tpmd/tpmd.sock ,
and in user mode
.IR "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tpmd/tpmd.sock" .
A socket inherited from systemd, if there is one, wins over both.
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " path"
Directory holding the key store. Defaults to
.I /var/lib/tpmd
in system mode and to the platform data directory
.RI ( "$XDG_DATA_HOME/tpmd" )
in user mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-key\-security " level"
The weakest custody the daemon will serve. One of
.BR file ,
.BR keyring ,
.BR sealed
or
.BR hardware .
Defaults to
.BR sealed .
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " name"
Force one backend. A machine is meant to keep the backend it chose; naming one
that contradicts the record is an error at start\-up rather than a silent
override.
.TP
.B \-\-system
Run as the shared system daemon. Implied by an effective uid of 0, which is why
the packaged unit passes it explicitly: it does not run as root.
.TP
.B \-\-user
Run as a per\-user agent.
.SS status
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Endpoint to reach. Defaults to the per\-user endpoint, then the system one.
.TP
.BI \-\-namespace " name"
Namespace to ask as. Keys are scoped by namespace, so this only changes which
namespace the agent reports on. Defaults to
.BR tpmd\-status .
.SS backend
.TP
.BI \-\-state\-dir " path"
Directory holding the record. Same default as
.BR serve .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TPMD_STATE_DIR
Overrides the state directory, in either mode. The packaged service also reads
it from
.IR /etc/default/tpmd .
.TP
.B TPMD_MIN_SECURITY
The default for
.BR \-\-min\-key\-security .
.TP
.B TPMD_TPM_DEVICE
Pin one TPM device node, for example
.IR /dev/tpmrm0 ,
instead of scanning for one.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log filter, in tracing's EnvFilter syntax.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where the per\-user socket is placed, as
.IR "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tpmd/tpmd.sock" .
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Where the per\-user store is placed, as
.IR "$XDG_DATA_HOME/tpmd/keys.sqlite" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/tpmd
Configuration for the packaged system service.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tpmd/keys.sqlite
The system store. It holds the device identity; deleting it destroys every key
this machine owns, and neither removal nor purge of the package does so.
.TP
.I /run/tpmd/tpmd.sock
The system socket, created by
.BR tpmd.socket .
.SH SECURITY
The system socket is world\-writable by design: any local user may connect, and
the daemon decides what each caller may do from the credentials the kernel
reports for the peer.
.PP
The daemon refuses operations below its security floor. It does not re\-key a
device to satisfy a request, and it does not downgrade a key that has been
lost: a lower level is a different key, or a refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tpmd-demo (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
